"id": "participles",
"el": "Μετοχές και γερούνδιο",
"title": "Participles and the gerund",
"span": "B1–B2",
"blurb": "The -οντας / -ώντας form (κάνοντας, μιλώντας) and the rule that it shares the subject of the main verb; -μένος versus -τός adjectives (κλεισμένος / κλειστός, ψημένος / ψητός); the learned participles in -όμενος, -ούμενος and -ων that fill the news and official Greek.",
"intro": [
{
"p": "Greek has fewer participles than English, and uses them more narrowly. The only active one in everyday speech is the indeclinable **-οντας / -ώντας** form (often called the gerund): **Μπήκε τρέχοντας.** \"He came in running.\" The passive **-μένος** is an ordinary adjective (chapter 27). The rest are learned forms you mainly need to recognise when reading."
},
